# db-backup — environment variable reference
#
# Copy the matching example into an ignored local file:
#   cp .env.example .env.development.local
#   cp .env.example .env.production.local
#
# NEVER commit .env.*.local. These files contain credentials and must be
# readable only by the owner (chmod 600): the doctor rejects symlinks and
# group/world-readable files before any other check.
# .env.production.local is MANDATORY; .env.development.local is optional.
# Process environment values fill variables absent from these files. If a
# variable exists in both with DIFFERENT values, the loader fails with a
# CONFLICT <VARIABLE> error naming it (values are never reported).
#
# `npm run doctor` (and `github:configure` before any GitHub call) checks BOTH
# existing files and requires all supported variables in every checked file
# (EXCEPT DECRYPT_KEY: it is restore-only, so backup-only setups may omit it
# and get a warning instead of a failure). Values come only from the dotenv
# file being checked: process exports are ignored by the doctor/configure.
# Legacy, duplicate, and unknown assignments only warn in the doctor and are
# never uploaded. Doctor reports are names-only — never values, URLs,
# passwords, age keys, or paths.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID and SUPABASE_CONFIG_PATH are the file-authoritative
# exceptions: the dotenv file value always wins, so a global shell export can
# neither redirect the R2 endpoint nor the destructive local-stack target. A
# present file that omits one FAILS (the export is not used) — see the
# precedence note above. Only when no dotenv file exists at all (CI) may the
# process export configure the run.

# GitHub Actions opt-in (repository configuration variable; reference only).
# Scheduled and manually dispatched backup jobs run only in a private repository
# when BACKUPS_ENABLED is exactly 'true'. `github:configure` sets this value
# at the repository level as its final step, after every environment upsert
# and legacy-secret deletion succeeded; a failed or partial run never enables
# backups. The doctor requires it in every checked file as an explicit local
# opt-in gate (exact lowercase `true`, no ambient fallback). Local commands do
# not consume it.
# BACKUPS_ENABLED=true

# Which environment this file belongs to. Must be 'development' or 'production'
# and must match the selected --environment flag (hosted commands).
# BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT=development
#
# `npm run backup:local` consumes only the fixed development identity
# (BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT must be 'development'): SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF and
# SUPABASE_CONFIG_PATH. Hosted DB, R2, age, and
# private identity fields are not resolved, validated, or returned for local
# backups. There is no environment selection: the local stack is a single
# database, and snapshots are always labeled `local` under
# `local-backups/local/`; the data source stays the local project stack. Row data is
# stored in PLAINTEXT gzip parts (no encryption). `npm run restore:development|
# restore:production --source local …` needs neither ENCRYPT_KEY (hosted-only)
# nor DECRYPT_KEY for plaintext snapshots (a legacy encrypted local snapshot
# still requires DECRYPT_KEY + age).

# Supabase project reference (20 lowercase alphanumeric characters).
# SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=

# Supabase Shared Session Pooler connection string. Copy the Session pooler
# value from Supabase Dashboard > Connect > Session pooler, e.g.
#   postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-<pool>-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
# Must use the postgresql:/postgres: scheme with sslmode require/verify-ca/
# verify-full and exactly the THIS project's reference in the username.
# NOTE: sslmode=require encrypts but does not authenticate the server; prefer
# verify-full (with the CA reachable by psql) on untrusted networks.
#
# The Shared Session Pooler supplies the IPv4 connectivity the default Docker
# bridge needs for Dockerized backup/restore clients. Direct URLs
# (db.<project-ref>.supabase.co) and transaction-mode port 6543 are
# deliberately rejected; so is the legacy SUPABASE_DB_URL variable name.
# SUPABASE_SHARED_POOLER_URL=
#
# Cloudflare account identifier (32 hexadecimal characters). The dotenv file
# value wins over any process-environment export (see precedence note above).
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# R2 bucket. MUST equal the selected environment exactly:
#   development -> development
#   production  -> production
# R2_BUCKET=

# R2 S3 API credentials (Object Read & Write, scoped to the matching bucket).
# Doctor-required canonical shapes: access key exactly 32 lowercase hex
# characters, secret key exactly 64 lowercase hex characters.
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Public age X25519 recipient for this environment (starts with 'age1...').
# Private age X25519 identity (AGE-SECRET-KEY-...). RESTORE-ONLY: the doctor
# never requires it (backup-only setups must not hold a private key) but
# validates the key-pair match whenever it IS present and warns when absent.
# Generate both with `npm run generate-age-keys`. ENCRYPT_KEY is backup-only
# (hosted); DECRYPT_KEY is required by r2/repo restores only. Never set the
# identity when only backing up; `--source local` snapshots are plaintext and
# never need it.
# ENCRYPT_KEY=
# DECRYPT_KEY=

# Exact supabase/config.toml file of the sibling main project, resolved
# relative to this repository when not absolute. The doctor requires it in
# BOTH files and validates it (readable <project>/supabase/config.toml,
# project_id, [db] port, Postgres major 17). Development selects the local
# stack when present, otherwise production does.
# REQUIRED by backup:local, reset:local, and the target side of
# restore:local; there is no default. The main-project root used as the
# Supabase CLI working directory is derived from this file.
# The legacy PROJECT_WORKDIR name is REJECTED by these commands (rename it;
# there is no fallback; the doctor only warns).
# SUPABASE_CONFIG_PATH=../main-project/supabase/config.toml
